course: dictionary
lesson: 5026
title: Ingredients
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An ingredient is one of the foods or things that are mixed together to make a dish. Flour and eggs are ingredients of a cake. The word can also mean one of the parts that make something succeed, like «the ingredients of a good story».
explain: «Even simple recipes from a bot can have mistakes, such as a missing ingredient.» A recipe with one ingredient missing may not turn out right, so always check the whole list before you start cooking.
explain: Context helps again. In a recipe, the ingredients come with amounts, so the words around the word tell you how much of each one to use. Food labels list ingredients too, and the list is the true story.
explain: «Pictures on packets are adverts; the ingredients list shows what is really inside.» A picture of strawberries on a box does not prove there are strawberries in it. Read the ingredients list to find out.
fact: An ingredient is a noun, and its plural is ingredients.
fact: In a recipe, ingredients are listed with amounts, such as 200 g of flour.
fact: Doubling a recipe means doubling every ingredient, and halving it means halving every one.
fact: On food labels, ingredients are listed from most to least, so the first one is the one there is most of.
fact: Labels also show allergens such as nuts, milk or gluten, so people can stay safe.
exercise: What is an ingredient? | *One of the foods mixed together to make a dish | A kind of oven | A tool for cutting vegetables | A picture on a box
exercise: If a recipe for four people is doubled, what happens to each ingredient? | None of them change | Only the flour is doubled | *Each one is doubled | Each one is halved
exercise: «Pictures on packets are adverts; the ingredients list shows what is really inside.» Where should you look to find out what is really in a packet? | The price sticker | The colour of the packet | *The ingredients list | Only the picture on the front
test: «Even simple recipes from a bot can have mistakes, such as a missing ingredient.» What might go wrong with a recipe that has a missing ingredient? | It will taste better | *The dish may not turn out right | It will make more food | The oven will switch itself off
test: On a food label, which ingredient is listed first? | The most expensive one | The rarest one | The one with the longest name | *The one there is the most of
exam: In a good story, which of these is one of the ingredients? | *A problem for the characters to solve | The number of pages | The size of the font | The price of the book
